      "sources": [
        {
          "label": "TS SDK workflow.ts — setDefaultSignalHandler + signal buffering impl comment",
          "locator": "https://github.com/temporalio/sdk-typescript/blob/main/packages/workflow/src/workflow.ts",
          "summary": "SDK sorts Signal/Update jobs ahead of other activation jobs; if no handler is registered the signal is pushed to an in-memory buffer. setDefaultSignalHandler dispatches buffered signals in server-acceptance order. Documentary contract: 'If a WFT contains a Signal/Update, and if a handler is available... then the handler will be executed' — i.e. the guarantee is conditional on a handler existing."
        },
        {
          "label": "Temporal community: How to handle undefined signals",
          "locator": "https://community.temporal.io/t/how-to-handle-undefined-signals/10692",
          "summary": "SDK maintainer (bergundy): 'if a handler isn't registered at the time a signal is received by the SDK, it is buffered in memory.' Recommends setDefaultSignalHandler as catch-all for visibility."
        },
        {
          "label": "Temporal docs — Errors: Unhandled Command + Pending Signals Limit Exceeded",
          "locator": "https://docs.temporal.io/references/errors",
          "summary": "UnhandledCommand = WFT failed because workflow attempted to close itself without handling new Events that arrived since WFT start. Transient; a retry WFT is scheduled. Pending Signals Limit Exceeded = workflow reached capacity for pending signals (server-side buffer cap)."
        },
        {
          "label": "Temporal API proto — WorkflowTaskFailedCause enum",
          "locator": "https://buf.build/temporalio/api/raw/54001d31c68a41dda97490fe01642301/-/temporal/api/enums/v1/failed_cause.proto",
          "summary": "UNHANDLED_COMMAND=1: 'Between starting and completing the workflow task (with a workflow completion command), some new command (like a signal) was processed into workflow history. The outstanding task will be failed with this reason, and a worker must pick up a new task.' PENDING_SIGNALS_LIMIT_EXCEEDED=28: server-side signal buffer cap."
        },
        {
          "label": "Temporal community: Continuously signalWithStart behaviour (maxim)",
          "locator": "https://community.temporal.io/t/continuously-signalwithstart-behaviour/16084",
          "summary": "Temporal maintainer (maxim): on race between completion and a new signal, server fails the last WFT with UnhandledCommand and rolls back so the signal is processed. If the signal arrives after the workflow completed at the service, a new execution is started (signalWithStart). 'signals cannot be lost if the workflow code is written correctly' — conditional on a handler existing."
        },
        {
          "label": "Temporal community: What happens to signals sent to a closing workflow",
          "locator": "https://community.temporal.io/t/what-happens-to-signals-sent-to-a-closing-workflow/8409",
          "summary": "Drain signal channel before exit; Temporal rolls back to last blocked state to give the workflow a chance to handle a signal arriving during the close window."
        },
        {
          "label": "Temporal community: Async drain signal & Unhandled Command (maxim)",
          "locator": "https://community.temporal.io/t/async-drain-signal-unhandled-command/11891",
          "summary": "'There is no way to eliminate UnhandledCommand as it is not an error but an indication of the race condition between workflow completion and new signals.' Benign when channel is drained correctly."
        },
        {
          "label": "TS SDK workflow-client.ts — query() routes to a worker that replays history",
          "locator": "https://github.com/temporalio/sdk-typescript/blob/main/packages/client/src/workflow-client.ts",
          "summary": "query() uses QueryWorkflow RPC; signal() resolves on server acceptance (does not wait for processing). QueryRejectedError thrown when queryRejectCondition rejects (e.g. workflow not open)."
        },
        {
          "label": "TS SDK interfaces.ts — query handlers are live read-only ops",
          "locator": "https://github.com/temporalio/sdk-typescript/blob/main/packages/workflow/src/interfaces.ts",
          "summary": "isReplayingHistoryEvents returns false during query handlers and update validators — 'live read-only operations that should not be considered as replaying history events.' Confirms queries execute on a live poller, not from stored history."
        },
        {
          "label": "Temporal docs — Handling Signals, Queries, & Updates",
          "locator": "https://docs.temporal.io/handling-messages",
          "summary": "Workflow execution is single-threaded; handlers run interleaved with main workflow. 'finish running all handlers before the Workflow run completes' via all_handlers_finished. Handler Unfinished Policy controls warnings for in-flight handlers."
        },
        {
          "label": "TS SDK common/workflow-options.ts — WorkflowIdReusePolicy + WorkflowIdConflictPolicy",
          "locator": "https://github.com/temporalio/sdk-typescript/blob/main/packages/common/src/workflow-options.ts",
          "summary": "ALLOW_DUPLICATE is default ReusePolicy (start allowed if previous is Closed). ConflictPolicy: FAIL / USE_EXISTING / TERMINATE_EXISTING for a RUNNING workflow with same ID."
        },
        {
          "label": "TS SDK common/failure.ts — WorkflowExecutionAlreadyStartedError",
          "locator": "https://github.com/temporalio/sdk-typescript/blob/main/packages/common/src/failure.ts",
          "summary": "Thrown when: conflict=FAIL and running; reuse=REJECT_DUPLICATE and closed; reuse=ALLOW_DUPLICATE_FAILED_ONLY and prior run Completed."
        },
        {
          "label": "Temporal docs — Worker Versioning (Deployments)",
          "locator": "https://docs.temporal.io/production-deployment/worker-deployments/worker-versioning",
          "summary": "Worker Deployment Version = deployment name + Build ID (operator-assigned free-form string). VersioningBehavior.PINNED pins a workflow to its current Build ID; AUTO_UPGRADE moves to latest. Server records versions when first poller arrives. Does NOT introspect registered handlers."
        },
        {
          "label": "TS SDK common/worker-deployments.ts — VersioningBehavior PINNED/AUTO_UPGRADE",
          "locator": "https://github.com/temporalio/sdk-typescript/blob/main/packages/common/src/worker-deployments.ts",
          "summary": "PINNED: workflow pinned to current Build ID unless manually moved. AUTO_UPGRADE: workflow moves to latest task-queue version on next task dispatch."
        },
        {
          "label": "TS SDK API — DescribeTaskQueueResponse (pollers, versioningInfo, versionsInfo)",
          "locator": "https://typescript.temporal.io/api/interfaces/proto.temporal.api.workflowservice.v1.IDescribeTaskQueueResponse",
          "summary": "pollers: IPollerInfo[] (includes identity). versioningInfo: routing target. versionsInfo: DEPRECATED, ENHANCED-mode only, per-Build-ID taskReachability. Reachability is eventually-consistent and conservative; reports open/new/closed reachability, NOT handler names."
        },
        {
          "label": "TS SDK API — GetWorkerTaskReachabilityRequest (deprecated)",
          "locator": "https://typescript.temporal.io/api/classes/proto.temporal.api.workflowservice.v1.GetWorkerTaskReachabilityRequest",
          "summary": "Deprecated: 'Use DescribeTaskQueue.' Returns whether a Build ID is reachable by open/closed/new workflows. taskReachability is eventually-consistent and takes the conservative side until convergence. Cannot report registered handlers."
        },
        {
          "label": "TS SDK API — DescribeWorkerDeploymentResponse / DescribeWorkerDeploymentVersionRequest",
          "locator": "https://typescript.temporal.io/api/classes/proto.temporal.api.workflowservice.v1.DescribeWorkerDeploymentResponse",
          "summary": "Worker Deployment API (versioning-3). Returns WorkerDeploymentInfo with VersionSummaries + DrainageStatus. Build IDs and deployment names are operator-assigned identifiers; the API tracks routing/drainage, not code-level handler registration."
        },
        {
          "label": "Temporal docs — Workers: Worker Identity",
          "locator": "https://docs.temporal.io/workers",
          "summary": "Worker Identity defaults to ${process.pid}@${os.hostname()}; operator-assigned free-form string visible in pollers list and event history. A tracing label, not a code-identity or handler-set proof."
        },
        {
          "label": "TS SDK worker-options.ts — buildId is operator-assigned",
          "locator": "https://github.com/temporalio/sdk-typescript/blob/main/packages/worker/src/worker-options.ts",
          "summary": "buildId: 'A string that should be unique to the exact worker code/binary being executed... Required if useVersioning is true.' It is a CLAIM by the operator, not server-introspected from code."
        },
        {
          "label": "Temporal issue #2860 — RequestCancel on completed returns OK",
          "locator": "https://github.com/temporalio/temporal/issues/2860",
          "summary": "RequestCancelWorkflowExecution against an already-completed workflow returns OK (no error). Cancel is best-effort by design; docs were corrected to drop the 'fails if completed' claim. Not usable as a 'only-if-still-X' guard."
        },
        {
          "label": "Temporal community: Race between signal handler and workflow completing",
          "locator": "https://community.temporal.io/t/race-condition-between-signal-handler-and-workflow-completing/16803",
          "summary": "Once the workflow function returns the workflow is completed and its activities are cancelled/not retried. Fix: await wf.condition(wf.allHandlersFinished) before return. Confirms single-threaded self-arbiter semantics."
        },
        {
          "label": "Temporal docs — Continue-As-New",
          "locator": "https://docs.temporal.io/workflow-execution/continue-as-new",
          "summary": "CaN completes current execution and atomically starts a new one with same Workflow ID, fresh Event History. 'Workflows that do this are often called Entity Workflows because they represent durable objects.' Bounds history, NOT running-workflow count."
        },
        {
          "label": "Temporal docs — Event History limits",
          "locator": "https://docs.temporal.io/workflow-execution/event",
          "summary": "Hard limits: 51,200 Events or 50MB history; >10,000 signals or >2,000 updates terminate the workflow. 'There is no time constraint on how long a Workflow Execution can run.' No per-namespace running-workflow COUNT cap documented."
        },
        {
          "label": "Temporal docs — Entity Workflow design pattern",
          "locator": "https://docs.temporal.io/design-patterns/entity-workflow",
          "summary": "Official recommendation for one-workflow-per-entity. 'Use entity ID as Workflow ID.' Must use Continue-As-New to prevent unbounded history. 'One Workflow per entity means you should consider costs at scale.' Not a fit for 'entities with only CRUD operations (use a database).'"
        },
        {
          "label": "Temporal docs — Entity lifecycle patterns",
          "locator": "https://docs.temporal.io/design-patterns/entity-lifecycle-patterns",
          "summary": "Entity Workflow + Continue-As-New + Updatable Timer are the official lifecycle patterns. CaN is the prescribed history-bound mechanism; nothing here prescribes completing-then-recreating idle entities."
        },
        {
          "label": "Temporal blog — Managing very long-running Workflows",
          "locator": "https://temporal.io/blog/very-long-running-workflows",
          "summary": "Entity Workflow = Workflows representing something that potentially lives forever. CaN is the mechanism to run forever. No guidance to hibernate idle entity workflows."
        },
        {
          "label": "Temporal docs — Cloud limits",
          "locator": "https://docs.temporal.io/cloud/limits",
          "summary": "Cloud limits: 20,000 activity + 20,000 workflow-task pollers per namespace; 2,000 incomplete in-flight commands per workflow execution; 51,200 events / 50MB history; APS/RPS/OPS rate limits. NO documented per-namespace cap on concurrent RUNNING workflow executions."
        },
        {
          "label": "Temporal docs — Cost optimization (Active vs Retained Storage)",
          "locator": "https://docs.temporal.io/best-practices/cost-optimization",
          "summary": "Active Storage (open workflows + their histories) is 'significantly more expensive' than Retained Storage. Missing Continue-As-New is the named cost anti-pattern. CaN before long sleeps 'closes the current execution (moving to cheaper Retained Storage) and starts fresh when work resumes.' Closest official analogue to the hibernation proposal — but CaN-based, not complete-and-recreate."
        },
        {
          "label": "Temporal community: How queries on completed workflows work",
          "locator": "https://community.temporal.io/t/how-queries-on-completed-workflows-work/11516",
          "summary": "Queries are answered by a worker replaying the (closed) history using current code — query results reflect the code of whichever worker served the query. Confirms query = point-in-time code-execution probe, not a stored artifact."
        },
        {
          "label": "sdk-core sticky_queues.md — query dispatch via poller",
          "locator": "https://github.com/temporalio/sdk-core/blob/master/arch_docs/sticky_queues.md",
          "summary": "Workers recreate state by replaying history then issue task completion; query tasks are dispatched through the same matching/poller path. Confirms queries execute on a live poller."
        }
      ],
      "architecture_assessment": "Three preconditions gate hibernation. (1) Running-worker signal-set identity has NO native Temporal API: DescribeTaskQueue/Worker-Versioning/Deployments expose operator-assigned Build IDs and eventually-consistent reachability, never the set of registered signal-handler names. A workflow QUERY is a viable point-in-time code-identity probe (queries execute on a live poller with isReplayingHistoryEvents=false) but is sound ONLY under PINNED worker versioning (workflow pinned to one build so query and subsequent signal route to the same code); without versioning it is a racy TOCTOU check. (2) Race-free self-completion IS cheaply satisfiable: a self-completing signal makes the single-threaded workflow the arbiter of its own close; the only residual race (signal arrives during the close window) is handled by server-side UnhandledCommand rollback, and is lossless WHEN a handler exists. Recreate-on-demand is cheap via WorkflowIdReusePolicy.ALLOW_DUPLICATE (default) + signalWithStart. (3) The premise that running-workflow COUNT is the thing to bound is questionable: Temporal has no per-namespace running-count cap, 191 idle workflows is operationally trivial, and the documented cost lever is Active Storage of open histories — which Continue-As-New already bounds and which the official Entity-Workflow + CaN pattern is designed to carry indefinitely. The deeper hibernation risk is silent data loss: a signal sent to a name NO worker build registers is buffered in SDK memory and is SILENTLY DISCARDED when the workflow completes (transient UnhandledCommand during the close race resolves to loss, not stuck); only setDefaultSignalHandler gives visibility. This loss is a real hazard for hibernation specifically because complete-then-recreate widens the window in which the host may emit a signal the (about-to-close / not-yet-recreated) workflow cannot handle.",
      "validation": {
        "status": "caution",
        "blockers": [],
        "notes": "Research succeeded; no source-access blocker. Caution (not fail) because no precondition is strictly unsatisfiable, but Q1's identity precondition is satisfiable only expensively (requires adopting PINNED worker versioning + an identity query + accepting a TOCTOU window), and Q3 shows the motivating premise (running count = cost driver) is weakly supported by documented limits — the cheaper, officially-blessed lever is Continue-As-New. Two material risks surface for the orchestrator to weigh: (a) silent signal loss on hibernation when the host emits a name no registered handler covers; (b) the identity-prevention check cannot be made lossless without versioning discipline."
      },
      "architecture_judgement": {
        "applicability": "applicable",
        "confidence": "high",
        "risk": "medium",
        "tradeoffs": [
          "Hibernation (complete + signalWithStart-recreate) reduces Active Storage cost of idle open workflows but introduces a silent-signal-loss window that Continue-As-New does not have (CaN preserves pending signals across the chain).",
          "A query-based running-worker identity probe is cheap to build but is sound only under PINNED worker versioning; adopting versioning is itself a non-trivial operational commitment (Build ID discipline, deployment management, drainage monitoring).",
          "Bounding running-workflow count addresses a metric (count) that Temporal does not limit per-namespace; the real cost lever (Active Storage GB) is already bounded by Continue-As-New, which the codebase appears to already use for terminal completion.",
          "Self-completing signals make close race-free w.r.t. workflow-internal state, but RequestCancel is best-effort and returns OK on already-completed workflows — it cannot serve as a 'complete only if still X' guard, so the design must use signal-driven self-close, not external cancel/terminate."
        ],
        "alternatives_considered": [
          {
            "option": "Continue-As-New with infrequent inactivity timers (official Entity-Workflow pattern)",
            "disposition": "preferred",
            "rationale": "Officially blessed for 'workflows that run forever.' Bounds history (the real storage cost), preserves pending signals across the chain (no silent loss), needs no identity probe. Downsides: does NOT reduce running-workflow count, and timers do not survive CaN (must be carried in input)."
          },
          {
            "option": "Hibernation (complete stale workflows + signalWithStart recreate on demand)",
            "disposition": "deferred",
            "rationale": "Reduces running count and Active Storage of truly-idle workflows. Costs: silent-signal-loss window (mitigated only by setDefaultSignalHandler + backward-compatible signal set), expensive/unsound identity check without versioning, and recreate-on-demand latency on first touch after idle. Defer until Active-Storage/APS cost evidence justifies the added risk."
          },
          {
            "option": "Hybrid: CaN for bounded-history long-life + hibernate only on a true inactivity timeout with default-signal-handler draining",
            "disposition": "deferred",
            "rationale": "Use CaN as the primary history bound; add hibernation only as an explicit inactivity-timeout transition that first drains via setDefaultSignalHandler (logging any unhandled name) before completing. Minimizes the loss window and makes any divergence observable. Defer until/unless hibernation is committed; then this is the strongest form."
          },
          {
            "option": "External database for entity state instead of one-workflow-per-entity",
            "disposition": "rejected",
            "rationale": "Temporal's own Entity-Workflow doc says CRUD-only entities should use a database. If ADV changes were mostly CRUD with occasional process logic, a DB + short-lived process workflows might be simpler — but this is a larger architectural change outside this change's scope and ADV's durable per-change workflow carries genuine process logic, so rejected for this context."
          }
        ],
        "recommendation": "Do NOT gate the design on a native running-worker-identity API — none exists. If hibernation is pursued: (1) adopt PINNED worker versioning so the identity probe (a registered query returning handler names) is sound, OR enforce a backward-compatible signal-set invariant (signal names are add-only across versions) so divergence cannot occur and the identity check becomes unnecessary; (2) implement close via a self-completing signal after await wf.condition(wf.allHandlersFinished), never via external RequestCancel/Terminate; (3) install setDefaultSignalHandler that logs every unhandled name BEFORE completing, so any divergence is observable rather than silently lost; (4) recreate with signalWithStart under WorkflowIdReusePolicy.ALLOW_DUPLICATE. Separately, re-examine whether 191 running workflows is a real cost problem — the documented levers (Active Storage, APS) point at Continue-As-New, not at count reduction."
      },
      "recommendation": "See architecture_judgement.recommendation. Summary: Q1 identity = NOT satisfiable natively, expensively satisfiable via versioned query-probe (recommend the add-only signal-set invariant instead, which dissolves the precondition). Q2 completion race = SATISFIABLE CHEAPLY via self-completing signal + allHandlersFinished + ALLOW_DUPLICATE/signalWithStart. Q3 = the cheaper answer is Continue-As-New (already in use for terminal completion); running-count bounding is solving a weakly-documented problem — verify the actual Active Storage / APS cost before committing to hibernation complexity.",
      "follow_ups": [
        "adv_change_show timed out (10s) on first call this session; session-health flagged the active change as possibly unsafe to continue from chat history. Orchestrator should resume decideWorkflowHibernation in a fresh session by changeId before relying on any in-flight context.",
        "Verify the codebase's actual signal-emission surface: enumerate every signal name the host (ADV plugin) sends to change workflows. If that set is add-only across all deployed worker builds, the Q1 identity precondition is dissolved for free and hibernation's data-loss risk collapses to 'only loses signals whose handlers were removed/renamed', which should be a release-gated invariant, not a runtime probe.",
        "Confirm whether ADV workers already use Worker Deployment Versioning (buildId/deploymentName). If not, the query-probe identity check is unsound and must not be relied upon.",
        "Measure the real cost driver before committing: pull Active Storage GB and APS for the ADV namespace. 191 open workflows is far below any documented Cloud limit; the decision should be evidence-led, not count-led.",
        "If hibernation proceeds, add a regression test that signals an unregistered name to a workflow in its close window and asserts the signal is observable via setDefaultSignalHandler (not silently dropped)."
      ]
    },
