        }
      ],
      "architecture_assessment": "The NEGATIVE verdict is architecturally sound and consistent with Temporal's canonical entity-workflow pattern: workflow lifetime mirrors entity lifetime, continue-as-new handles history longevity, completion happens only at entity-terminal. Four of five shape arguments (#1 entity-mirror-correct, #2 cache-vs-mirror regression, #4 no Temporal guidance to complete idle entities, #5 real defect is entity-lifecycle discipline) are well-supported by official Temporal docs and stand independently. DEC-1's bundle-generation proxy is verified correct at the code level for signal-SET equality (worker-bundle-manifest.ts:29,81-89 hashes workflows.js, which carries every wf.setHandler). DEC-2's self-completion-by-signal is the correct single-threaded arbiter pattern, and RequestCancel returning OK on completed workflows is confirmed (temporal #2860). DEC-3/DEC-4/DEC-5 are appropriately scoped. HOWEVER, shape argument #3 contains a load-bearing factual inaccuracy: it asserts continue-as-new 'serializes pending signals into the next run' and is 'strictly safer' on signal grounds. Temporal docs and ADV's own code contradict this — continue-as-new ALSO drops undrained/unregistered signals unless the workflow manually carries them (ADV does not; it carries only a state seed at workflows.ts:2286). The actual architectural difference is liveness (continue-as-new keeps the entity able to receive future signals; completion ends reception), not signal preservation. Must be restated before ADR approval; correction strengthens the design by making #3 consistent with DEC-5.",
      "validation": {
        "status": "caution",
        "blockers": [
          {
            "finding": "Shape argument #3 asserts 'Completion discards signals; continue-as-new preserves them' and 'Continue-as-new serializes pending signals into the next run,' concluding continue-as-new is 'strictly safer than hibernation' on signal-preservation grounds. Factually inaccurate. Temporal's continue-as-new docs state unprocessed signals are LOST on continue-as-new unless manually drained and carried as args; the loyalty guide's 'preserved' wording is achieved by manual state serialization, not automatic SDK behavior; TS SDK continueAsNew() takes args only with no signal auto-carry; setDefaultSignalHandler.dispatchBufferedSignals() is same-run not next-run. ADV's own code (workflows.ts:2285-2288) carries a state seed via buildChangeWorkflowContinueAsNewSeed, not pending/unregistered signals, and BOTH terminal and continue-as-new paths drain only registered handlers via allHandlersFinished. For the DEC-5 unregistered-signal case, completion and continue-as-new BOTH discard. Violates DONT2 (asserts a mechanism guarantees a property — signal preservation — without tracing declaration through consumer to observable effect; the trace refutes it) and undermines the precision AC3/SC2 require of the architectural-shape basis. Does NOT overturn the verdict — arguments #1,#2,#4,#5 independently justify NEGATIVE. Accurate restatement: continue-as-new preserves entity LIVENESS (future signal reception) while completion ends it; neither automatically preserves undrained unregistered signals (exactly why DEC-5/rq-hib04 is verdict-independent).",
            "contract_ids": [
              "DONT2",
              "AC3",
              "SC2"
            ],
            "scope": "in_scope",
            "in_scope_remediation": "Restate shape argument #3 in design.md (the deliverable of this decision-only change, so fully in-scope). Replace the signal-preservation framing with the liveness framing: 'Both completion and continue-as-new drop undrained/unregistered signals; ADV mitigates registered signals via allHandlersFinished in both paths. The architectural difference is that continue-as-new preserves the entity's ability to RECEIVE future signals (keeps the durable mirror alive), while completing a live entity (hibernation) ends reception and requires recreation via signalWithStart. This liveness asymmetry — not a signal-serialization guarantee — is why continue-as-new is the sanctioned longevity mechanism.' Also makes #3 consistent with DEC-5 and removes the false 'strictly safer on signal grounds' premise. No verdict change, no new scope.",
            "source": {
              "label": "Temporal continue-as-new docs + ADV workflows.ts:2285-2288",
              "locator": "https://docs.temporal.io/design-patterns/continue-as-new ('Any Signals received but not yet processed are lost when Continue-As-New starts a fresh execution'); https://docs.temporal.io/develop/typescript/workflows/message-passing; plugin/src/temporal/workflows.ts:2285-2288 (continueAsNew carries state seed, not signals)",
              "summary": "Continue-as-new does not automatically preserve/serialize pending or unregistered signals; ADV's implementation carries state only. Claim #3's signal-preservation rationale is unfounded; true architectural difference is liveness, not preservation."
            }
          }
        ],
        "notes": "Verdict (NEGATIVE) is sound and survives correction of claim #3. Spec-law compliance PASS: no existing spec requires a positive hibernation decision; C2 (signal/query-only, no defineUpdate) satisfied by DEC-2's self-completion-by-signal and enforced by advance-delivery spec + workflow-bundle-boundary.test.ts; DEC-4's recording of an unresolved mechanism satisfies C3 (separates established fact from unestablished mechanism) and violates no evidence/verification spec law because it explicitly declines to assert the mechanism. DDC1-DDC5 well-formed and traceable. Revisit trigger measurable (SQL join on history_node bytes x executions_visibility.status=1; DDC5 pins the method; note it is dev-server-sqlite-specific). required_validation_consistency.status = caution. Alternatives disposed: (a) namespace retention/archival orthogonal (closed-history, freelist_count=0 healthy) acceptably implicit; (b) lower continue-as-new threshold correctly disposed; (c) scheduled reaper IS the preferred alternative; (d) multi-namespace split omitted, acceptable for decision-only shape ADR, revisit trigger implicitly covers."
      },
      "architecture_judgement": {
        "applicability": "applicable",
        "confidence": "high",
        "risk": "medium",
        "tradeoffs": [
          "Recording a contested 5-round decision with full evidence anchors (DEC-1..5, DDC1-5) adds document weight but is justified by DONT1/DONT3's anti-re-litigation objective; a thinner record would invite a seventh round.",
          "Inline ADR (vs docs/adr/) matches ADR-0001/0003 precedent and keeps the decision adjacent to the change, at the cost of discoverability for readers who scan docs/adr/.",
          "Negative verdict accepts bounded growth (71.9MB, 0.8-1.6MB/day) until entity-lifecycle discipline happens; mitigated by a measurable revisit trigger but real if discipline stalls.",
          "DEC-4 leaves orphan mechanism unresolved by design (C3); correct for a decision-only change but means 93.5MB reclamation deferred with no owner-bound timeline.",
          "Revisit-trigger thresholds (500MB / 10MB-day-over-30d) are measurable on the local dev-server sqlite but would need restating for a real Temporal deployment; acceptable since ADV targets the local server topology."
